WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Hello everyone, this video is a companion piece to the documentary In Search of a Flat Earth.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:10.480
n this video I’m going to cover the curvature experiment I did for that video at Lake Minnewanka, Alberta,

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:15.100
and I'll go a bit slower, cover all the details, and include a lot more footage.

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:18.680
First I will go over the parameters of the experiment and then, following that,

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:23.300
will include most of the footage from the test itself, lightly trimmed for viewability.

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:32.400
All the relevant footage for this project was recorded using the Blackmagic Pocket 4K in its 2.6k 16mm sensor crop mode.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:39.300
For the purpose of field of view calculations we’ll be assuming a sensor area of 12.5mm by 7mm.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:47.600
Additionally the primary test footage was shot at 120 frames per second to maximize detail, with a shutter speed of 180°,

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:51.120
resulting in an exposure interval of 4 milliseconds.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:56.840
For this experiment we are standing on the southwest bank of Minnewanka, right near the SCUBA access,

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:02.840
looking northeast towards Aylmer canyon, a distance of almost exactly 7km.

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:10.320
Just to get a sense of what we will be looking at, here is what the location looks like on the Google Streetview from the tour boat.

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:19.020
And here is footage of the shoreline from the beach looking west down the coast.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:29.040
We’re on a 16mm lens here giving us a horizontal field of view of 42.5° and a vertical of 24.68°.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:36.300
Comparing the resulting image with a 42.5° arc overlaid on satellite images confirms this.

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:41.300
With that we can then look at other footage of the north shore and compare relative sizes,

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:44.480
noting that the north beach is actually reasonably steep in many places,

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:47.120
particularly towards the mouth of Aylmer canyon,

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:53.620
with an elevation gain of up to 1 metre from the water’s edge to the forest.

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:59.880
As another point of reference, here is the Minnewanka tour boat, seen at a distance of approximately 1km.

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:03.180
Using the depth markings on the side of the boat as a reference,

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:09.400
we can confirm that the height of the boat from the water’s surface to the top of the rear canopy is around 3 metres,

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:15.400
with the distinct blue/green canopy being approximately 1.5 metres tall at the front of the boat,

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.660
and 2 metres tall at the back.

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:26.380
For the primary test we used an old Tamron FD zoom lens with an operational focal length of 288mm confirmed in controlled tests.

00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:30.000
This gives us a field of view of 2.48°,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.060
with a field width at a distance of 7km of about 302m.

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:38.640
Checking this using these two points, half the field-width apart,

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:42.680
against satellite maps, both place them 150m apart.

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:50.020
With this confirmed we can thus confidently operate off a field height of 170m at the plane of the beach.

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:54.720
With the camera mounted on the jib the maximum useful elevation above the water’s surface is

00:02:54.720 --> 00:03:00.180
around 1.5 metres, with the minimum elevation being approximately 10 centimetres.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:03.120
This gives us the known variables of distance and viewing height,

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:09.780
allowing us to test the assumption that the earth is a convex sphere with a radius of 6,371km.

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:12.700
We will also be assuming that light travels in straight lines.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:17.960
Credit to github user Dizzib who has compiled all this math into a convenient online calculator.

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:23.180
The two variables we are interested in are the distance to the local horizon, d1,

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:26.860
and the hidden-object-height at our destination, h1.

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:28.620
Using Pythagoras

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:33.320
D1 = root H0 squared + 2 R H0

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:40.360
And H1 equals root D0 minus D1 squared plus R squared minus R

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:47.680
This may seem intimidating at first glance, but at the core we’re just solving two right-angle triangles.

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:50.340
Completing for our maximum and minimum heights

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:53.980
we should expect to see the occlusion at a distance of 7km

00:03:53.980 --> 00:04:02.900
go from 0.54m at the peak to 2.7m at the nadir, a delta of 2.2m.

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:06.380
Using our field height calculation of 170m,

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:10.940
2.2m elevation should correspond to 1.3% of the image height,

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:13.920
a space indicated by the two red lines.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:18.320
And then in motion, comparing the change in visibility between peak and nadir

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:22.340
with this space and known-objects, notably the tour boat,

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:25.620
we are able to observe a delta of over 2 metres,

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:33.080
supporting the assumption that the earth is a convex sphere with a radius of approximately 6,371 km.

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:35.080
[chill music]

